                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "So I played the original Kumoon around 2005, the same year it released. I have been trying to find it again for 20 years. Along the way of finally finding it, I discovered this iteration, which seems to be a continuation/successor of the original game. Here's how I think it stacks up to the original after a wee bit of playtime.\n\nIn the 2005 version of Kumoon, you played as a chick - the animal - with a revolver, a shotgun, and a bazooka. Levels featured a pretty sterile aesthetic, containing a series of red boxes that turn white if disturbed by bullets, other white boxes, or you. Disturbing a box granted you some points, but using your weapons deducted points and could bring your score into the negatives - as did disturbing the red boxes with your own body - so the trick was to ricochet your shots off of walls and other boxes in order to bring down more of them at a time. These ricochets would also grant you extra points to further offset the score reduction from shooting. It's a pretty decent free game, and I had a lot of fun giving it another go in 2026.\n\nIn this 2015 version of Kumoon, nearly every aspect I mentioned has been reworked. You are now a robot with what I think is only one \"weapon\" where you shoot singular balls. Here, the ricochets are the defining mechanic, and you don't lose any points for shooting or colliding with boxes with your body. Accumulating ricochets with your projectile - without firing another - will increase the amount of points each box offers, with the goal being to beat a \"par\" score. The aesthetic is now this more coherent, cutesy sci-fi look, which I like just a little bit less than the original style for reasons I'll get into soon.\n\nOne thing I do like here is that there are little screens on the wall that show you your current points, the current music playing, and your FPS. I assume this is partially for the VR mode, but it appears in non-VR as well, and I think it's a cool touch. Oh yeah, I forgot to mention that this game has a VR mode! I have not tested it myself, but it seems to have been made before hand-tracking controllers were a thing, so you have to play it with a regular controller. Make of that what you will!\n\nOverall, I think Kumoon is a pretty good puzzler for the asking price, and having a VR mode is pretty cool too. I do miss the vibe and \"art style\" of the original 2005 version though... it really reveled in its own weirdness, where the 2015 version feels almost too sanded-off. I would say the updated mechanics are a lot more approachable though.",
